# SYNOPSIS

```swift
enum Interrupt {
  enum Mode: UInt32, Sendable {
    case `default` = 0
    case edgeLow = 2
    case edgeHigh = 4
    case levelLow = 6
    case levelHigh = 8
  }

  static var packetType: UInt32 { get }
  static var msiSystemBase: UInt64 { get }

  static func create(resource: borrowing Handle, number: UInt32,
                     mode: Mode = .default) throws(Status) -> Handle
  static func bind(_ interrupt: borrowing Handle, port: borrowing Handle,
                   key: UInt64) throws(Status)

  @discardableResult
  static func ack(_ interrupts: [UInt32], statuses: inout [Status])
    throws(Status) -> Int
  static func ack(_ interrupt: borrowing Handle) throws(Status)

  static func msiAllocate(resource: borrowing Handle, count: Int)
    throws(Status) -> Handle
  static func msi(allocation: borrowing Handle, id: UInt32,
                  vmo: borrowing Handle, offset: Int, msix: Bool)
    throws(Status) -> Handle
  static func msiInfo(_ allocation: borrowing Handle) throws(Status)
    -> MSIInfo
  static func setAffinity(_ interrupt: borrowing Handle, cpus: UInt64)
    throws(Status)
}

struct MSIInfo: Equatable, Sendable {
  var targetAddress: UInt64
  var targetData: UInt32
  var baseIRQ: UInt32
  var count: UInt32
  var interrupts: UInt32
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

An interrupt object stands for a device's interrupt, identified by its number: a GSI on amd64 and rv64, an INTID on arm64.
It is made under an interrupt resource that covers the number (see sys-resource(2)).
The way to wait for one is a port bound to it: packets arrive until `ack` re-arms the interrupt, and a level-triggered interrupt stays masked from when it fires until it is acknowledged.

`create` makes the interrupt object for `number`.
`mode` says how the line signals: `default` is what firmware says (on amd64, the MADT's overrides, else edge-high for ISA and level-low for PCI), and the others set the polarity and trigger.
It is `interrupt_create(2)`.

`bind` sends packets to `port` with `key` when the interrupt fires.
A port that interrupts may be bound to is made with `Port.create(bindToInterrupt: true)`.
A packet has type `packetType`, and `payload.0` is the time stamp.
See sys-port(2).

`ack(_:statuses:)` re-arms several interrupts in one call, given their raw handle numbers.
An interrupt thread acknowledges every vector it has handled at once.
It writes each interrupt's status to `statuses` and returns how many succeeded.
`ack(_:)` re-arms one and throws that status if it failed.
It is `interrupt_ack(2)`.

`setAffinity` routes the interrupt to one of the CPUs in the mask `cpus`, bit n being CPU n (`interrupt_set_affinity(2)`).

## MSI

`msiAllocate` allocates `count` contiguous message-signalled interrupt vectors, a power of two up to 32, under the system resource whose base is `msiSystemBase`.
It works on amd64 only.
`msi` makes the interrupt object for vector `id` of an allocation.
With `msix` true, `vmo` holds the device's MSI-X table at `offset`, as physical uncached memory, and the kernel programs and masks entry `id`.
With `msix` false, `vmo` is the function's configuration space and `offset` is where the MSI capability is.
`msiInfo` returns what a device must write to raise the allocation's interrupts: interrupt id n writes `targetData + n` to `targetAddress`.
`baseIRQ` and `count` describe the allocation, and `interrupts` is how many of its ids have an interrupt object.
A driver for a plain MSI capability programs the function from these values.
These are `msi_allocate(2)` and `msi_create(2)`.

